{"name":"Aquamarine Blue","hex":"#71d9e2","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"aquamarine","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The bluish-green colour of the sea.","A transparent bluish-green, sometimes yellow-green, variety of beryl."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a bluish-green colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/aquamarine"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Aquamarine (gem)","blocks":[{"text":"Aquamarine is a pale-blue to light-green variety of the beryl family. It is transparent to translucent and possesses a hexagonal crystal system. Aquamarine is a fairly common gemstone, rendering it more accessible for purchase, compared to other gems in the beryl family."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Aquamarine_P1000141.JPG/330px-Aquamarine_P1000141.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aquamarine_(gem)","wikidata":"Q58959"},{"title":"Aquamarine (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Aquamarine is a pale blue-green color between cyan and cerulean on the color wheel. It is named after the mineral aquamarine, a gemstone mainly found in granite rocks. The first recorded use of aquamarine as a color name in English was in 1598."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2a/Aquamarine.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aquamarine_(color)","wikidata":"Q372973"},{"title":"Blue","b